# ===========================================================================
#  What this repository does not carry.
#
#  The rule, and it is the whole of the rule:
#
#      If it cannot go into a public repository, it does not LIVE in the
#      repository. It lives outside the tree and something inside the tree
#      says where.
#
#  So this file is left holding two honest categories: files a command
#  regenerates, and other people's papers. It does not hold "the 308 MB of
#  therapy audio we would rather not push" -- that is not in here at all, it
#  is at ~/phi/PSYCH-ASR/, and research/PSYCH-ASR/README.md says so. An ignore
#  rule is a guard somebody can delete by accident; a directory outside the
#  repository is a guard nothing in here can undo.
#
#  EACH WORKSPACE KEEPS ITS OWN .gitignore, and those are the load-bearing
#  ones. In particular the `live/*` allowlist -- cards, slate, answers,
#  archive, inbox, text, state.json, turns.jsonl tracked, the rest ignored --
#  is what makes a lecture the same lesson on whichever machine picks it up.
#  Nothing here may shadow one of those: git will not descend into a directory
#  ignored higher up, so a rule for `live/` written HERE would make every
#  deeper `!live/cards/` unreachable. That is why this file names no directory
#  a workspace re-includes.
# ===========================================================================

# ---- build products, every language in here ----
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
node_modules/
*.log

# Lean's compiled Mathlib: 14 GB across 150,582 files, and `lake build`
# recreates all of it. It never leaves the machine.
.lake/
build/

# ---- LaTeX, which writes eight files beside every one it is asked for ----
*.aux
*.nav
*.snm
*.toc
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz

# ---- the picture of what somebody drew on a meeting slide ----
# `meetings/meeting.tex`, its PDF and its page map are tracked: the deck is the
# document. `meetings/marks/` is not -- it is the rendered ink, copied out of
# the serving board's own `live/annotations/` (which IS tracked) so that the
# turn woken by it has a path it can open, and it is cleared whenever the deck
# is replaced. A buffer between one machine's board and one machine's turn.
meetings/marks/

# ---- other people's published papers ----
# The citation libraries hold PDFs whose copyright belongs to their authors.
# The INDEXES are tracked -- README.md, CITATION_MAP.md, the role-grouped
# manifests -- so a clone arrives with the bibliography described but not
# carried. Anything that grows a reference shelf gets the same treatment.
**/references/*.pdf

# ---- editor and OS cruft ----
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*~
.*.swp
.vscode/

# ---- one vendor's config directory, none of which is this repository's ----
# NOTHING under an assistant's own directory is tracked here, and the test is
# that deleting every one of them costs `bash ai-config/scripts/install.sh` and
# nothing else.
#
# What used to be tracked was three `settings.json` permission allowlists --
# which commands may run unattended in a workspace. That is a fact about the
# workspace and worth keeping; the JSON schema around it is one provider's and
# is not. Both live in `ai-config` now: the lists in `workspaces/*.allow`,
# naming no vendor, and the schema in a renderer the assistant's own descriptor
# points at. `install.sh` folds them into that assistant's USER-LEVEL settings,
# outside this tree entirely, so nothing installs anything here at all. The
# rule below is therefore absolute rather than a filter: no directory named for
# an assistant belongs in this repository, and one appearing is this machine's
# own state rather than anything the repository needs.
#
# The same rule covers a workflow, a subagent, a slash command and a skill,
# which are logic written against one provider's API and go stale at its next
# release. A job worth keeping is kept as a specification any assistant can
# execute -- `board/tools/docs-audit.md` is the pattern -- and the harness is
# regenerated from it.
#
# `**/` on purpose: a pattern with a slash in it is anchored to the directory
# its .gitignore sits in, so `.claude/` written HERE would match only the one
# at the repository root and silently miss the nine inside the workspaces --
# which are the only ones that actually exist.
**/.claude/

# ---- other people's BOOKS, and every excerpt cut out of one ----
# A course keeps its set text on disk and reads it; a public repository does not
# redistribute it. `scripts/split-textbook.sh` says of its own output "they are
# derived artifacts and git-ignored" -- which was the intent from the start and
# was never actually enforced, so forty chapter excerpts and three whole
# textbooks were tracked. The excerpts regenerate from the book in one command.
#
# What IS tracked is everything the person wrote about the reading: the notes,
# the homework, the typeset transcripts, chapters.tsv. That is the work.
**/textbook/*.pdf
**/chapters/*/reading/ch*.pdf
**/chapters/*/reading/ch*.txt

# ---- the assistant configuration, which is its own private repository ----
# `ai-config/` lives INSIDE this tree and is tracked by its own git, not by
# this one. It holds the operating contract every assistant reads, the PHI
# guard, and each vendor's settings files -- and it stays private because those
# settings name real paths on the lab's storage and the guard describes what it
# is guarding.
#
# Inside the tree so that one directory is the whole of the work and a machine
# is set up by one clone plus one command; ignored here so that a public
# repository never carries it. `ai-config/README.md` says how it is linked into
# place, and Atlas's own README names it as a thing that exists and is
# deliberately elsewhere.
/ai-config/
